theory:
## All Parameters Are Required

In XIOM, every parameter you declare MUST be provided when calling the function. There are no optional parameters or default values.

```xiom
fn greet(name: Str, punctuation: Str) -> Str {
  return "Hello, " + name + punctuation;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  // Both arguments must be provided
  let msg = greet("Kilo", "!");  // "Hello, Kilo!"

  // greet("Kilo");  // ERROR: missing second argument!
  return 0;
}
```

### The Wrapper Pattern

Since there are no defaults, create a wrapper function for common cases:

```xiom
// General function — caller provides everything
fn greet_with(name: Str, greeting: Str, punctuation: Str) -> Str {
  return greeting + ", " + name + punctuation;
}

// Wrapper for the most common case
fn greet(name: Str) -> Str {
  return greet_with(name, "Hello", "!");
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let msg1 = greet("Kilo");                          // Hello, Kilo!
  let msg2 = greet_with("Kilo", "Good morning", "."); // Good morning, Kilo.
  return 0;
}
```

### Why No Defaults?

XIOM's philosophy: be explicit. Every argument is intentional. This prevents bugs where a default value is used accidentally.

### Design Tips

```xiom
// Instead of many parameters with 'defaults', use focused functions:
fn create_window(title: Str, width: Int, height: Int) -> Int {
  return width * height;  // simplified
}

fn create_small_window(title: Str) -> Int {
  return create_window(title, 400, 300);
}

fn create_large_window(title: Str) -> Int {
  return create_window(title, 1024, 768);
}
```

Clear names beat hidden defaults every time.

